WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:06.686 --> 00:10.776 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference May 3,

00:11.006 --> 00:16.646 align:middle
2023 CHAIR POWELL.

00:16.646 --> 00:17.226 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:18.046 --> 00:21.296 align:middle
Before discussing today's
meeting, let me comment briefly

00:21.296 --> 00:23.156 align:middle
on recent developments
in the banking sector.

00:24.616 --> 00:26.966 align:middle
Conditions in that sector
have broadly improved

00:26.966 --> 00:29.936 align:middle
since early March, and the
U.S banking system is sound

00:30.046 --> 00:30.646 align:middle
and resilient.

00:32.206 --> 00:34.566 align:middle
We will continue to monitor
conditions in the sector.

00:34.566 --> 00:37.436 align:middle
We are committed to learning the
right lessons from this episode

00:37.616 --> 00:41.096 align:middle
and will work to prevent events
like these from happening again.

00:41.406 --> 00:45.796 align:middle
As a first step in that process,
last week we released Vice Chair

00:45.796 --> 00:47.626 align:middle
for Supervision Barr's Review

00:48.136 --> 00:50.296 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve's
Supervision and Regulation

00:50.296 --> 00:51.446 align:middle
of Silicon Valley Bank.

00:52.846 --> 00:55.816 align:middle
The review's findings underscore
the need to address our rules

00:55.856 --> 00:58.556 align:middle
and supervisory practices
to make for a stronger

00:58.586 --> 01:00.096 align:middle
and more resilient
banking system,

01:00.096 --> 01:01.686 align:middle
and I am confident
that we will do so.

01:03.946 --> 01:05.526 align:middle
From the perspective
of monetary policy,

01:05.526 --> 01:08.026 align:middle
our focus remains squarely
on our dual mandate

01:08.116 --> 01:10.806 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

01:10.806 --> 01:11.706 align:middle
for the American people.

01:13.666 --> 01:15.516 align:middle
My colleagues and I
understand the hardship

01:15.586 --> 01:19.316 align:middle
that high inflation is causing,
and we remain strongly committed

01:19.316 --> 01:22.046 align:middle
to bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

01:24.176 --> 01:25.916 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

01:25.916 --> 01:26.676 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve.

01:27.756 --> 01:28.836 align:middle
Without price stability,

01:28.916 --> 01:30.926 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

01:31.876 --> 01:33.516 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

01:33.516 --> 01:36.026 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

01:36.026 --> 01:38.656 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

01:40.666 --> 01:43.296 align:middle
Today, the FOMC raised
its policy interest rate

01:43.356 --> 01:44.706 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point.

01:45.636 --> 01:48.236 align:middle
Since early last year, we have
raised interest rates by a total

01:48.236 --> 01:50.716 align:middle
of 5 percentage points in
order to attain a stance

01:50.716 --> 01:53.606 align:middle
of monetary policy that is
sufficiently restrictive

01:53.636 --> 01:55.926 align:middle
to return inflation to
2 percent over time.

01:56.626 --> 02:01.056 align:middle
We are also continuing to
reduce our securities holdings.

02:02.046 --> 02:04.456 align:middle
Looking ahead, we will take
a data-dependent approach

02:04.456 --> 02:05.686 align:middle
in determining the extent

02:05.686 --> 02:08.216 align:middle
to which additional policy
firming may be appropriate.

02:09.206 --> 02:12.586 align:middle
I will have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

02:12.796 --> 02:15.386 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

02:15.676 --> 02:20.086 align:middle
The U.S. economy slowed
significantly last year,

02:20.216 --> 02:24.656 align:middle
with real GDP rising at a
below-trend pace of 0.9 percent.

02:26.566 --> 02:28.536 align:middle
The pace of economic
growth in the first quarter

02:28.536 --> 02:31.516 align:middle
of this year continued to
be modest, at 1.1 percent,

02:31.896 --> 02:33.686 align:middle
despite a pickup in
consumer spending.

02:35.006 --> 02:37.366 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector remains weak,

02:37.506 --> 02:40.016 align:middle
largely reflecting higher
mortgage, mortgage rates.

02:41.366 --> 02:44.186 align:middle
Higher interest rates and
slower output growth also appear

02:44.186 --> 02:46.426 align:middle
to be weighing on
business fixed investment.

02:47.196 --> 02:50.396 align:middle
The labor market
remains very tight.

02:50.856 --> 02:52.416 align:middle
Over the first three
months of the year,

02:52.476 --> 02:56.326 align:middle
job gains averaged
345,000 jobs per month.

02:57.256 --> 02:59.266 align:middle
The unemployment rate
remained very low

02:59.266 --> 03:00.996 align:middle
in March, at 3.5 percent.

03:02.086 --> 03:04.906 align:middle
Even so, there are some
signs that supply and demand

03:04.906 --> 03:07.446 align:middle
in the labor market are coming
back into better balance.

03:09.096 --> 03:10.966 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate has moved

03:10.966 --> 03:12.566 align:middle
up in recent months,
particularly

03:12.566 --> 03:15.736 align:middle
for individuals aged
25 to 54 years.

03:16.936 --> 03:19.906 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
sown-shown some signs of easing,

03:19.906 --> 03:22.356 align:middle
and job vacancies have
declined so far this year.

03:23.646 --> 03:27.126 align:middle
But overall, labor demand still
substantially exceeds the supply

03:27.126 --> 03:28.146 align:middle
of available workers.

03:32.086 --> 03:35.726 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:36.396 --> 03:37.896 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in March,

03:37.936 --> 03:40.816 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 4.2 percent;

03:41.686 --> 03:43.846 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:43.996 --> 03:46.756 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
4.6 percent.

03:48.006 --> 03:51.056 align:middle
Inflation has moderated somewhat
since the middle of last year.

03:51.626 --> 03:54.336 align:middle
Nonetheless, inflation
pressures continue to run high,

03:55.026 --> 03:57.096 align:middle
and the process of
getting inflation back

03:57.096 --> 03:59.446 align:middle
down to 2 percent
has a long way to go.

04:00.716 --> 04:02.036 align:middle
Despite elevated inflation,

04:02.126 --> 04:04.276 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations appear

04:04.276 --> 04:07.676 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

04:07.676 --> 04:11.296 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

04:11.836 --> 04:13.836 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:16.716 --> 04:19.306 align:middle
The Fed's monetary policy
actions are guided by,

04:19.386 --> 04:22.096 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:22.186 --> 04:24.166 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

04:25.406 --> 04:27.256 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

04:27.416 --> 04:30.436 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

04:30.606 --> 04:34.406 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

04:34.406 --> 04:36.956 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

04:37.246 --> 04:38.506 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

04:39.996 --> 04:41.436 align:middle
We are highly attentive
to the risks

04:41.436 --> 04:44.616 align:middle
that high inflation pose-poses
to both sides of our mandate,

04:45.186 --> 04:47.416 align:middle
and we are strongly committed
to returning inflation

04:47.756 --> 04:49.136 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective.

04:51.736 --> 04:52.556 align:middle
At today's meeting,

04:52.716 --> 04:55.066 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

04:55.116 --> 04:57.826 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point,
bringing the target range

04:57.826 --> 04:59.496 align:middle
to 5 to 5¼ percent.

05:00.166 --> 05:01.836 align:middle
And we're continuing to
[carry out] the process

05:01.926 --> 05:04.436 align:middle
of significantly reducing
our securities holdings.

05:05.866 --> 05:07.736 align:middle
With today's action, we
have raised interest rates

05:07.776 --> 05:09.866 align:middle
by 5 percentage points in
a little more than a year.

05:10.446 --> 05:13.386 align:middle
We are seeing the effects of
our policy tightening on demand

05:13.536 --> 05:15.546 align:middle
in the most interest
rate-sensitive sectors

05:15.546 --> 05:18.146 align:middle
of the economy, particularly
housing and investment.

05:19.366 --> 05:21.736 align:middle
It will take time, however,
for the full effects

05:21.816 --> 05:23.776 align:middle
of monetary restraint
to be realized,

05:24.046 --> 05:25.266 align:middle
especially on inflation.

05:27.066 --> 05:31.226 align:middle
In addition, the economy is
likely to face further headwinds

05:31.226 --> 05:32.766 align:middle
from tighter credit conditions.

05:33.996 --> 05:35.876 align:middle
Credit conditions had
already been tightening

05:35.876 --> 05:39.176 align:middle
over the past year or so in
response to our policy actions

05:39.466 --> 05:40.956 align:middle
and a softer economic outlook.

05:42.576 --> 05:44.546 align:middle
But the strains that
emerged in the banking sector

05:44.546 --> 05:46.866 align:middle
in early March appear
to be resulting

05:46.866 --> 05:48.376 align:middle
in even tighter credit
conditions

05:48.376 --> 05:49.646 align:middle
for households and businesses.

05:50.216 --> 05:54.256 align:middle
In turn, these tighter credit
conditions are likely to weigh

05:54.256 --> 05:56.646 align:middle
on economic activity,
hiring, and inflation.

05:57.436 --> 05:59.596 align:middle
The extent of these
effects remains uncertain.

06:01.806 --> 06:03.346 align:middle
In light of these
uncertain headwinds,

06:03.416 --> 06:06.026 align:middle
along with the monetary policy
restraint we have put in place,

06:06.676 --> 06:10.466 align:middle
our future policy actions will
depend on how events unfold.

06:11.756 --> 06:12.876 align:middle
In determining the extent

06:12.876 --> 06:15.566 align:middle
to which additional policy
firming may be appropriate

06:15.566 --> 06:17.796 align:middle
to return inflation to
2 percent over time,

06:18.806 --> 06:19.556 align:middle
the Committee will take

06:19.556 --> 06:22.376 align:middle
into account the cumulative
tightening of monetary policy,

06:23.116 --> 06:26.226 align:middle
the lags with which monetary
policy affects economic activity

06:26.226 --> 06:29.496 align:middle
and inflation, and economic
and financial developments.

06:30.706 --> 06:32.746 align:middle
We, we will make that
determination meeting

06:32.746 --> 06:35.946 align:middle
by meeting, based on the
totality of incoming data

06:35.946 --> 06:37.846 align:middle
and their implications
for the outlook

06:37.846 --> 06:39.696 align:middle
for economic activity
and inflation.

06:40.836 --> 06:42.086 align:middle
And we are prepared to do more

06:42.446 --> 06:44.816 align:middle
if greater monetary policy
restraint is warranted.

06:45.016 --> 06:49.186 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

06:49.186 --> 06:50.446 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

06:50.986 --> 06:53.836 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

06:54.016 --> 06:54.646 align:middle
well anchored.

06:55.856 --> 06:58.206 align:middle
Reducing inflation is
likely to require a period

06:58.206 --> 07:01.056 align:middle
of below-trend growth
and some softening

07:01.056 --> 07:02.236 align:middle
of labor market conditions.

07:03.236 --> 07:06.696 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

07:06.696 --> 07:08.196 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

07:08.616 --> 07:10.496 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

07:12.186 --> 07:15.776 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:15.936 --> 07:17.966 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:18.816 --> 07:21.216 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:22.286 --> 07:24.116 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:24.116 --> 07:25.656 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:25.736 --> 07:27.066 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:27.686 --> 07:28.036 align:middle
Thank you.

07:28.136 --> 07:29.216 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:29.976 --> 07:30.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:30.476 --> 07:31.646 align:middle
Thanks. Jeanna.

07:31.736 --> 07:32.256 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

07:34.566 --> 07:38.146 align:middle
Hi. Jeanna Smialek,
New York Times.

07:38.146 --> 07:40.206 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

07:40.206 --> 07:41.836 align:middle
I wonder if you could tell
us whether we should read the

07:41.836 --> 07:45.116 align:middle
statement today as a suggestion
that the Committee is prepared

07:45.116 --> 07:47.096 align:middle
to pause interest rate
increases in June.

07:47.096 --> 07:50.096 align:middle
And I also wonder
if the Fed staff has

07:50.096 --> 07:53.076 align:middle
in any way revised their
forecast for a mild recession

07:53.076 --> 07:56.496 align:middle
from the March minutes and,
if so, what a recession

07:56.496 --> 07:58.416 align:middle
like what they're
envisioning would look and feel

07:58.416 --> 08:00.996 align:middle
like when it comes to, for
example, the unemployment rate.

08:02.496 --> 08:02.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:02.986 --> 08:06.686 align:middle
So, taking your question-of
course, today our decision was

08:06.686 --> 08:09.236 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate by 25 basis points.

08:09.716 --> 08:12.926 align:middle
A, a decision on a pause
was not made today.

08:13.966 --> 08:18.396 align:middle
You will have noticed that in
the-in the statement from March,

08:18.396 --> 08:20.946 align:middle
we had a sentence that said
the Committee anticipates

08:20.946 --> 08:23.326 align:middle
that some additional policy
firming may be appropriate.

08:24.066 --> 08:27.056 align:middle
That sentence is, is not in,
in the statement anymore.

08:27.056 --> 08:28.816 align:middle
We took that out, and
instead we're saying that,

08:29.216 --> 08:30.526 align:middle
in determining the extent

08:30.526 --> 08:32.836 align:middle
to which additional policy
firming may be appropriate

08:32.836 --> 08:34.476 align:middle
to return inflation to
2 percent over time,

08:34.946 --> 08:37.006 align:middle
the Committee will take into
account certain factors.

08:37.006 --> 08:39.736 align:middle
So we-that's a-that's
a meaningful change

08:39.736 --> 08:41.956 align:middle
that we're no longer
saying that we anticipate.

08:42.466 --> 08:43.936 align:middle
And so we'll be driven

08:43.936 --> 08:45.936 align:middle
by incoming data
meeting by meeting.

08:45.976 --> 08:47.656 align:middle
And, you know, we'll approach

08:47.656 --> 08:54.386 align:middle
that question at
the June meeting.

08:54.486 --> 09:00.506 align:middle
So the-the staff's forecast
is-so let me say-start by saying

09:00.506 --> 09:03.756 align:middle
that that's not my
own most likely case,

09:03.756 --> 09:06.156 align:middle
which is really that, that
the economy will continue

09:06.156 --> 09:08.316 align:middle
to grow at, at a
modest rate this year.

09:08.886 --> 09:11.336 align:middle
And I think that's-so
different people

09:11.336 --> 09:12.536 align:middle
on the Committee have
different forecasts.

09:12.536 --> 09:14.836 align:middle
That's, that's my own assessment
of the most likely path.

09:15.336 --> 09:18.086 align:middle
[The] staff produces its own
forecast, and it's independent

09:18.086 --> 09:20.386 align:middle
of the forecasts of,
of the participants,

09:21.016 --> 09:21.926 align:middle
which include the Governors

09:21.926 --> 09:23.556 align:middle
and the Reserve Bank
presidents, of course.

09:23.936 --> 09:25.676 align:middle
And we think this is a
healthy thing-that the,

09:25.676 --> 09:27.906 align:middle
the staff is writing down
what they really think.

09:27.966 --> 09:29.366 align:middle
They're not especially
influenced

09:29.366 --> 09:31.056 align:middle
by what the Governors
think, and vice versa.

09:31.316 --> 09:33.626 align:middle
The Governors are not
taking what the staff says

09:33.626 --> 09:34.586 align:middle
and just writing that down.

09:34.586 --> 09:37.926 align:middle
So it's actually good
that the, the staff

09:37.926 --> 09:41.716 align:middle
and individual participants can
have different perspectives.

09:43.426 --> 09:47.566 align:middle
So, broadly, the forecast
was for a mild recession,

09:47.566 --> 09:50.836 align:middle
and by that I would characterize
as one in which the rise

09:50.836 --> 09:53.456 align:middle
in unemployment is smaller
than is-has been typical

09:53.456 --> 09:55.806 align:middle
in modern-era recessions.

09:56.086 --> 10:01.746 align:middle
I wouldn't want to characterize
the staff's forecast

10:01.746 --> 10:02.466 align:middle
for this meeting.

10:02.466 --> 10:04.486 align:middle
We'll, we'll leave that to
the minutes-but broadly,

10:04.546 --> 10:05.966 align:middle
broadly similar to that.

10:05.966 --> 10:07.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:07.716 --> 10:11.546 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

10:11.766 --> 10:12.556 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

10:12.556 --> 10:14.066 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

10:14.066 --> 10:15.156 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

10:15.226 --> 10:18.306 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can
talk about the account

10:18.306 --> 10:21.246 align:middle
of possible effects of
a debt-limit standoff.

10:21.246 --> 10:23.516 align:middle
You've said repeatedly that
the ceiling must be raised,

10:23.516 --> 10:26.016 align:middle
but do you see any
economics effects

10:26.016 --> 10:27.676 align:middle
of even getting close
to a default?

10:28.046 --> 10:29.856 align:middle
And what type of situation
would that look like?

10:30.496 --> 10:31.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:32.336 --> 10:34.136 align:middle
So I wouldn't want to
speculate specifically,

10:34.136 --> 10:37.246 align:middle
but I will say this: These
are fiscal policy matters,

10:37.246 --> 10:39.586 align:middle
for starters, and they're,
they're for Congress

10:39.586 --> 10:41.506 align:middle
and the Administration-for
the elected parts

10:41.506 --> 10:42.616 align:middle
of the government to deal with.

10:42.826 --> 10:46.196 align:middle
And they're really
consigned to them.

10:46.726 --> 10:49.956 align:middle
From our standpoint, I would
just say this: It's essential

10:49.956 --> 10:52.496 align:middle
that the debt ceiling be
raised in a timely way,

10:52.926 --> 10:54.676 align:middle
so that the U.S.
government can pay all

10:54.676 --> 10:55.886 align:middle
of its bills when they're due.

10:56.506 --> 10:58.566 align:middle
A failure to do that
would be unprecedented.

10:59.436 --> 11:02.466 align:middle
We'd be in uncharted territory,
and the-and the consequences

11:02.466 --> 11:05.716 align:middle
to the U.S. economy would be
highly uncertain and could,

11:05.766 --> 11:07.426 align:middle
could be quite a[d]verse.

11:07.526 --> 11:09.776 align:middle
So I'll just leave it there.

11:09.776 --> 11:11.896 align:middle
We, we don't give
advice to either side.

11:12.366 --> 11:16.476 align:middle
We just would point out that
it-that it's very important

11:16.476 --> 11:17.036 align:middle
that this be done.

11:17.036 --> 11:19.626 align:middle
And the, the other point
I'll make about that, though,

11:19.626 --> 11:20.966 align:middle
is that no one should assume

11:21.646 --> 11:24.346 align:middle
that the Fed can protect the
economy from the potential,

11:24.706 --> 11:27.516 align:middle
you know, short- and
long-term effects of a failure

11:27.516 --> 11:28.606 align:middle
to pay our bills on time.

11:28.876 --> 11:32.976 align:middle
We, we-it's, it would be
so uncertain that it's just

11:32.976 --> 11:35.596 align:middle
as important that, that
this-we never get to a place

11:35.596 --> 11:38.626 align:middle
where we're actually talking
about or even having a situation

11:38.626 --> 11:40.236 align:middle
where the U.S. government's
not paying its bills.

11:40.236 --> 11:40.916 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

11:40.916 --> 11:41.756 align:middle
And just a follow-up.

11:41.936 --> 11:43.856 align:middle
Was discussion around
the uncertainty

11:43.856 --> 11:45.406 align:middle
of a possible standoff-did

11:45.406 --> 11:47.386 align:middle
that affect today's monetary
policy decision at all?

11:47.476 --> 11:47.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:47.756 --> 11:48.606 align:middle
I wouldn't say that it did.

11:48.606 --> 11:50.236 align:middle
It was-of course, it's
something that came up.

11:50.236 --> 11:52.906 align:middle
We talk a lot about risks
to the-to the outlook,

11:52.906 --> 11:54.356 align:middle
and that will-that come up.

11:54.356 --> 11:56.986 align:middle
A number of people did raise
that as a risk to the outlook.

11:56.986 --> 11:58.176 align:middle
I wouldn't say that
it was important

11:58.176 --> 12:00.156 align:middle
in today's monetary
policy decision.

12:02.116 --> 12:03.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:04.496 --> 12:06.126 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

12:06.186 --> 12:10.256 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, can
you-oh, thank you.

12:10.256 --> 12:11.546 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

12:12.046 --> 12:16.716 align:middle
Can you tell us what the Federal
Reserve Board did in the wake

12:16.716 --> 12:19.276 align:middle
of that February presentation
where you were informed

12:19.276 --> 12:20.606 align:middle
that Silicon Valley Bank

12:20.986 --> 12:22.826 align:middle
and other banks were
experiencing interest

12:22.856 --> 12:23.536 align:middle
rate risks?

12:24.096 --> 12:26.516 align:middle
And can you tell me what
supervisory actions you've done

12:26.516 --> 12:30.446 align:middle
in the wake of the recent
bank failures to make sure

12:30.446 --> 12:33.196 align:middle
that banks are currently
appropriately managing interest

12:33.196 --> 12:33.606 align:middle
rate risk?

12:33.606 --> 12:36.736 align:middle
And kind of part 3, but it's
all the same question here-do,

12:36.796 --> 12:39.666 align:middle
do you still think this
separation principle

12:39.666 --> 12:40.876 align:middle
that monetary policy

12:41.336 --> 12:43.566 align:middle
and supervision can be
handled with different tools?

12:43.566 --> 12:43.936 align:middle
Thank you.

12:44.186 --> 12:44.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:44.526 --> 12:48.286 align:middle
Sure. So the February 14
presentation-I didn't remember

12:48.286 --> 12:48.676 align:middle
it very well.

12:48.676 --> 12:49.846 align:middle
But now, of course,
I've gone back

12:49.846 --> 12:51.176 align:middle
and looked at it very carefully.

12:51.346 --> 12:52.096 align:middle
I did remember it.

12:52.686 --> 12:54.856 align:middle
And what it was was a
general presentation.

12:54.856 --> 12:56.496 align:middle
It was an informational briefing

12:56.496 --> 12:58.026 align:middle
of the whole Board-the
entire Board.

12:58.026 --> 12:59.126 align:middle
I think all members were there.

12:59.716 --> 13:01.866 align:middle
And it was about
interest rate risk

13:01.866 --> 13:05.566 align:middle
in the banks and,
and lots of data.

13:05.566 --> 13:07.566 align:middle
And there was one page
on Silicon Valley Bank,

13:07.616 --> 13:09.986 align:middle
which talked about, you know,

13:10.026 --> 13:12.946 align:middle
the amount of losses they-or
mark-to-market losses they had

13:12.946 --> 13:13.646 align:middle
in their portfolio.

13:13.646 --> 13:16.396 align:middle
There was nothing in it
about-that I recall, anyway,

13:16.396 --> 13:18.966 align:middle
about, about the
risk of a bank run.

13:18.966 --> 13:22.536 align:middle
So it was-I think the takeaway
was, they were going away

13:22.596 --> 13:25.676 align:middle
to do a-an assessment,
a vertical-sorry,

13:25.676 --> 13:28.146 align:middle
horizontal assessment
of, of banks.

13:28.726 --> 13:32.456 align:middle
It wasn't-it, it wasn't
presented as an urgent

13:32.456 --> 13:33.926 align:middle
or alarming situation.

13:33.926 --> 13:36.486 align:middle
It was presented as
a-as an informational,

13:36.486 --> 13:38.466 align:middle
nondecisional kind of a thing.

13:38.466 --> 13:40.946 align:middle
And I thought it was a
good presentation and, and,

13:40.946 --> 13:42.986 align:middle
as I said, did remember it.

13:42.986 --> 13:45.096 align:middle
In terms of what
we're doing-of course,

13:45.346 --> 13:48.806 align:middle
I think banks themselves are,
are-many, many banks are now,

13:48.806 --> 13:53.626 align:middle
are attending to liquidity and
taking opportunity now, really,

13:53.626 --> 13:59.026 align:middle
since, since the events of, of
early March, to build liquidity.

13:59.026 --> 14:02.476 align:middle
And you asked about the
separation principle.

14:02.476 --> 14:06.336 align:middle
I-you know, like so many
things, it, it's very useful.

14:07.096 --> 14:10.906 align:middle
But, you know, ultimately,
it has its limits.

14:10.906 --> 14:14.026 align:middle
I mean, I, I think in this
particular case, we have found

14:14.076 --> 14:16.616 align:middle
that the monetary policy tools

14:16.616 --> 14:18.936 align:middle
and the financial stability
tools are not in conflict.

14:18.936 --> 14:20.626 align:middle
They're both-they're
working well together.

14:20.626 --> 14:24.346 align:middle
We've used our, our financial
stability tools to support banks

14:24.406 --> 14:25.626 align:middle
through our lending facilities.

14:26.286 --> 14:27.956 align:middle
And, at the same
time, we've been able

14:27.956 --> 14:30.036 align:middle
to use our monetary policy tools

14:30.036 --> 14:32.056 align:middle
to foster maximum employment
and price stability.

14:33.696 --> 14:35.116 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:35.176 --> 14:37.356 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I'm sorry.

14:37.596 --> 14:39.966 align:middle
I don't mean to be
argumentative, but the,

14:40.066 --> 14:41.736 align:middle
the staff report said, "SVB .

14:41.926 --> 14:43.976 align:middle
. . has significant
interest rate risk."

14:43.976 --> 14:45.976 align:middle
It said, "Interest rate
risk measurement[s] .

14:45.976 --> 14:46.746 align:middle
. . failed" at SVB.

14:46.746 --> 14:49.156 align:middle
And it said, "Banks with
large unrealized losses face

14:49.156 --> 14:50.776 align:middle
significant safety
and soundness risks."

14:50.776 --> 14:52.366 align:middle
Why was that not alarming?

14:52.736 --> 14:53.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:54.276 --> 14:55.826 align:middle
Well, I mean, I didn't
say it wasn't alarming.

14:55.826 --> 14:58.566 align:middle
It was-they're pointing out
something that they're working

14:58.566 --> 15:00.426 align:middle
on and that they're on the
case-that, that, you know,

15:00.426 --> 15:03.296 align:middle
that-I'm not sure whether they
mentioned-I think they did,

15:03.296 --> 15:03.626 align:middle
actually.

15:03.626 --> 15:07.306 align:middle
They mentioned that they
had taken regulatory action

15:07.306 --> 15:09.076 align:middle
matter-or supervisory
action in the form

15:09.076 --> 15:10.846 align:middle
of matters requiring attention.

15:11.326 --> 15:13.096 align:middle
So I think that was also
in the presentation.

15:13.576 --> 15:15.756 align:middle
I think it was to say:
Yes, this is a bank,

15:15.756 --> 15:16.686 align:middle
and there are many other banks

15:16.686 --> 15:19.976 align:middle
that are experiencing this-these
things, and we're on the case.

15:20.986 --> 15:21.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:21.306 --> 15:22.386 align:middle
Let's go to Victoria.

15:22.386 --> 15:24.406 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

15:25.986 --> 15:26.796 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

15:26.796 --> 15:30.416 align:middle
I wanted to ask-obviously,
with the recent bank turmoil,

15:30.416 --> 15:33.996 align:middle
we've seen multiple
banks buy other banks.

15:33.996 --> 15:35.816 align:middle
And I was just curious
whether you think

15:35.816 --> 15:37.596 align:middle
that further consolidation

15:37.596 --> 15:39.506 align:middle
in the banking sector
would increase

15:39.766 --> 15:41.476 align:middle
or decrease financial stability

15:41.476 --> 15:43.716 align:middle
and whether you have any
concerns about the biggest bank

15:43.716 --> 15:45.336 align:middle
in the U.S. getting even larger.

15:46.226 --> 15:46.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:46.576 --> 15:49.786 align:middle
So I, you know, we certainly
don't-and I don't have an agenda

15:49.786 --> 15:52.006 align:middle
to further consolidate banks.

15:52.006 --> 15:54.286 align:middle
There's been-consolidation
has been a factor

15:54.286 --> 15:57.956 align:middle
in the U.S. banking industry
really since interstate banking

15:57.956 --> 16:00.236 align:middle
and before that even-it goes
back more than 30 years.

16:00.236 --> 16:02.306 align:middle
You-when I was in the
government a while back,

16:02.306 --> 16:03.826 align:middle
I think there were 14,000 banks.

16:03.826 --> 16:05.686 align:middle
Now there are 4,000 and change.

16:05.686 --> 16:07.336 align:middle
So that's, that's going on.

16:07.336 --> 16:11.746 align:middle
I personally have long felt
that having small-, medium-,

16:11.746 --> 16:14.096 align:middle
and large-size banks
is a, a great part

16:14.176 --> 16:15.576 align:middle
of our banking system.

16:15.576 --> 16:20.176 align:middle
You know, the community banks
serve particular customers very

16:20.176 --> 16:24.116 align:middle
well; regional banks serve
very important purposes,

16:24.116 --> 16:26.556 align:middle
and the various kinds
of G-SIBs do as well.

16:26.556 --> 16:30.416 align:middle
So I think it's healthy to have
a, you know, an arrange-a range

16:30.416 --> 16:32.306 align:middle
of different kinds of banks
doing different things.

16:32.986 --> 16:35.896 align:middle
I think that's a positive thing.

16:36.966 --> 16:39.326 align:middle
Is it a financial-so
I would just say,

16:39.326 --> 16:43.566 align:middle
in terms of J.P. Morgan
buying First Republic,

16:44.186 --> 16:49.586 align:middle
the FDIC really runs
the process of closing

16:49.726 --> 16:51.746 align:middle
and selling a closed
bank completely.

16:51.746 --> 16:52.746 align:middle
That, that is their role,

16:52.746 --> 16:55.636 align:middle
so I really don't have a
comment on, on that process.

16:56.136 --> 16:57.406 align:middle
As you know, there's
an exception

16:57.406 --> 16:59.996 align:middle
to the deposit cap
for a failing bank.

17:00.476 --> 17:01.406 align:middle
So it was legitimate.

17:01.406 --> 17:05.646 align:middle
And I think the FDIC, I believe,
is bound by law to take the bid

17:05.646 --> 17:07.106 align:middle
that is the least-cost bid.

17:07.906 --> 17:09.506 align:middle
So I would assume
that's what they did.

17:09.956 --> 17:10.426 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

17:10.426 --> 17:12.036 align:middle
So do you have any concerns
about the fact that they're,

17:12.086 --> 17:13.136 align:middle
they're getting larger
in general?

17:13.766 --> 17:14.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:15.096 --> 17:17.826 align:middle
So I, I think it's probably
good policy that we,

17:17.956 --> 17:21.416 align:middle
we don't want the largest
banks doing big acquisitions.

17:22.106 --> 17:23.086 align:middle
That is the policy.

17:23.286 --> 17:26.146 align:middle
And-but this is-this is an
exception for a failing bank.

17:26.146 --> 17:27.906 align:middle
And I, I think it's
actually a good outcome

17:27.906 --> 17:28.766 align:middle
for the banking system.

17:29.026 --> 17:31.516 align:middle
It also would have-would have
been a good-a good outcome

17:31.516 --> 17:32.666 align:middle
for the banking system had one

17:32.666 --> 17:35.426 align:middle
of the regional banks
bought, bought this company.

17:35.426 --> 17:36.586 align:middle
And that could have
been the outcome.

17:36.586 --> 17:40.366 align:middle
But, ultimately, we have to
follow the law in our agencies,

17:40.366 --> 17:43.866 align:middle
and the law is, it goes to
the, the least-cost bid.

17:43.866 --> 17:45.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:45.366 --> 17:45.836 align:middle
Now to Colby.

17:46.706 --> 17:49.186 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

17:49.706 --> 17:50.676 align:middle
Thank you.

17:50.676 --> 17:52.686 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

17:52.836 --> 17:55.036 align:middle
At the March meeting, you
mentioned that tightening

17:55.036 --> 17:57.756 align:middle
of credit conditions from the
recent bank stress could be

17:57.756 --> 18:00.246 align:middle
equal to one or more
rate increases.

18:00.486 --> 18:02.206 align:middle
So, given developments
since then,

18:02.446 --> 18:03.846 align:middle
how has your estimate changed?

18:04.346 --> 18:04.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:05.646 --> 18:09.206 align:middle
Yeah. I think-I think I follow
that up by saying, it's,

18:09.206 --> 18:12.586 align:middle
it's quite impossible to
have a precise estimate

18:12.686 --> 18:13.886 align:middle
of the words to that effect.

18:14.396 --> 18:16.566 align:middle
But, in principle,
that's the idea.

18:16.566 --> 18:18.596 align:middle
You know, when we-we've
been raising interest rates,

18:18.596 --> 18:21.506 align:middle
and that raises the price of
credit, and that, in a sense,

18:21.506 --> 18:23.796 align:middle
restricts credit in the economy,

18:23.846 --> 18:25.196 align:middle
working through the
price mechanism.

18:25.556 --> 18:28.506 align:middle
And, you know, when banks
raise their credit standards,

18:28.746 --> 18:30.826 align:middle
that can also make
credit tighter in a kind

18:30.826 --> 18:32.076 align:middle
of broadly similar way.

18:32.076 --> 18:36.436 align:middle
It isn't-it isn't possible to
make a kind of clean translation

18:36.436 --> 18:39.536 align:middle
between one and the other,
although firms are trying

18:39.536 --> 18:40.886 align:middle
that and, you know,
we're trying it.

18:40.886 --> 18:43.916 align:middle
But, ultimately, we have
to be-we have to be honest

18:43.916 --> 18:47.056 align:middle
and humble about our ability
to make a precise assessment.

18:47.056 --> 18:50.366 align:middle
So it does complicate the
task of achieving, you know,

18:50.476 --> 18:52.316 align:middle
a sufficiently restrictive
stance.

18:52.716 --> 18:55.706 align:middle
But I think, conceptually,
though, we think that, you know,

18:55.926 --> 18:59.456 align:middle
interest rates-in
principle, we won't have

18:59.456 --> 19:01.156 align:middle
to raise the rates quite as high

19:01.156 --> 19:02.976 align:middle
as we would have had
this not happened.

19:03.416 --> 19:05.986 align:middle
The extent of that
is so hard to predict

19:05.986 --> 19:07.886 align:middle
because we don't know
how persistent these,

19:08.216 --> 19:09.676 align:middle
these effects will be.

19:09.676 --> 19:10.856 align:middle
We don't know how
large they'll be

19:10.856 --> 19:12.366 align:middle
and how long they'll
take to be transmitted.

19:12.366 --> 19:15.276 align:middle
But that's, that's what we'll be
watching carefully to find out.

19:15.886 --> 19:16.186 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

19:16.186 --> 19:18.826 align:middle
Just to quickly follow up-what
does it suggest about the scope

19:18.826 --> 19:22.316 align:middle
for the Committee to pause
rate increases perhaps as early

19:22.316 --> 19:25.906 align:middle
as next month, even if the
data remains strong then, if,

19:25.906 --> 19:27.636 align:middle
if it's having some kind
of substitute effect?

19:27.796 --> 19:29.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:29.526 --> 19:31.766 align:middle
It's that-this is just
something that we have to factor

19:31.766 --> 19:35.736 align:middle
in as we-as we want
to find ourselves.

19:35.736 --> 19:40.306 align:middle
So I guess I would say it
this way: The assessment of,

19:40.496 --> 19:44.046 align:middle
of the extent to which
additional policy firming may be

19:44.046 --> 19:47.076 align:middle
appropriate is going to be an
ongoing one, meeting by meeting,

19:47.076 --> 19:48.526 align:middle
and we're going to be
looking at the factors

19:48.526 --> 19:50.156 align:middle
that I mentioned
that-they're listed in,

19:50.186 --> 19:52.536 align:middle
in the statement,
the obvious factors.

19:53.026 --> 19:55.486 align:middle
That's, that's the way we're
going to be thinking about it.

19:56.006 --> 19:58.806 align:middle
And that's really all we can do.

19:58.806 --> 20:01.826 align:middle
As I say, it does complicate-we,
we have, you know,

20:02.146 --> 20:04.216 align:middle
a broad understanding
of monetary policy.

20:04.216 --> 20:06.406 align:middle
Credit tightening is
a different thing.

20:06.636 --> 20:07.966 align:middle
There's a lot of
literature on that.

20:08.046 --> 20:12.866 align:middle
But translating it into,
into rate hikes is uncertain,

20:12.866 --> 20:14.456 align:middle
let's say-it adds even
further uncertainty.

20:14.456 --> 20:17.046 align:middle
Nonetheless, we'll be able
to see what's happening

20:17.046 --> 20:18.806 align:middle
with credit conditions-what's
happening with lending.

20:19.156 --> 20:20.826 align:middle
We get-there's a
lot of data on that.

20:20.906 --> 20:22.256 align:middle
And, you know, we'll,
we'll factor

20:22.256 --> 20:23.296 align:middle
that into our decisionmaking.

20:23.296 --> 20:24.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:24.386 --> 20:27.036 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:27.036 --> 20:28.006 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

20:28.006 --> 20:28.426 align:middle
Thank you.

20:28.426 --> 20:31.016 align:middle
So, noting that the statement
dropped the reference

20:31.076 --> 20:34.696 align:middle
to "sufficiently"
restricted-"restrictive,"

20:35.036 --> 20:37.536 align:middle
I was wondering, given
your baseline outlook,

20:37.586 --> 20:40.696 align:middle
whether you feel this current
rate of 5 to 5¼ percent is,

20:40.696 --> 20:42.326 align:middle
in fact, sufficiently
restrictive.

20:42.566 --> 20:42.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:42.836 --> 20:44.386 align:middle
That's going to be an
ongoing assessment.

20:44.386 --> 20:46.476 align:middle
We're going to need
data to accumulate

20:46.476 --> 20:50.916 align:middle
on that-not an assessment
that we've made, as-that,

20:50.916 --> 20:53.056 align:middle
that would mean we think
we've reached that point.

20:53.056 --> 20:55.586 align:middle
And I just think it's,
it's not possible to say

20:55.586 --> 20:57.616 align:middle
that with confidence now.

20:57.946 --> 21:02.046 align:middle
But, nonetheless, you, you
will know that the Summary

21:02.046 --> 21:06.746 align:middle
of Economic Projections from the
March meeting showed that in-at

21:06.746 --> 21:09.546 align:middle
that point in time, that the
median participant thought

21:09.546 --> 21:13.126 align:middle
that this was-this was
the appropriate level

21:13.126 --> 21:14.726 align:middle
of the-of the ultimate
high level of rates.

21:15.336 --> 21:15.996 align:middle
We don't know that.

21:15.996 --> 21:17.806 align:middle
We'll revisit that
at the June meeting.

21:18.416 --> 21:20.226 align:middle
And that's-you know,
we're just going

21:20.226 --> 21:23.036 align:middle
to have to-before we
really declare that,

21:23.036 --> 21:29.026 align:middle
I think we're going to have to
see data accumulating and, and,

21:29.026 --> 21:30.516 align:middle
you know, make that-as
I mentioned,

21:30.516 --> 21:31.556 align:middle
it's an ongoing assessment.

21:31.556 --> 21:32.356 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

21:32.356 --> 21:33.316 align:middle
I have a follow-up on credit.

21:33.536 --> 21:37.326 align:middle
Could you give us a sense of
what the SLOOS survey indicated?

21:37.326 --> 21:39.716 align:middle
It was already, I think, 40,

21:39.716 --> 21:41.996 align:middle
45 percent of banks
were tightening credit

21:41.996 --> 21:44.156 align:middle
as of the, the last survey.

21:44.156 --> 21:46.536 align:middle
What did this one show,
and how did that weigh

21:46.756 --> 21:47.966 align:middle
into your deliberations?

21:47.966 --> 21:48.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:48.376 --> 21:51.726 align:middle
So we're going to release the
results of this SLOOS on May 8,

21:51.826 --> 21:53.486 align:middle
in line with our
usual time frame.

21:53.486 --> 21:56.676 align:middle
And I would just say that the
SLOOS is broadly consistent,

21:56.676 --> 21:59.256 align:middle
when you see it, with how we
and others have been thinking

21:59.256 --> 22:01.726 align:middle
about the situation and what
we're seeing from other sources.

22:02.256 --> 22:04.056 align:middle
You will have seen the
Beige Book and listened

22:04.056 --> 22:06.306 align:middle
to the various earnings
calls that indicate

22:06.306 --> 22:08.426 align:middle
that midsize banks have-some

22:08.426 --> 22:10.286 align:middle
of them had been tightening
their lending standards.

22:10.836 --> 22:14.996 align:middle
Banking data will show that
lending has continued to grow,

22:14.996 --> 22:16.436 align:middle
but the pace has
been slowing really

22:16.436 --> 22:17.876 align:middle
since the second
half of last year.

22:18.806 --> 22:19.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:19.976 --> 22:23.206 align:middle
Let's go to Nick.

22:23.756 --> 22:25.076 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

22:25.076 --> 22:26.456 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, Wall
Street Journal.

22:27.116 --> 22:29.596 align:middle
Chair Powell, the argument
around the end of last year

22:29.596 --> 22:31.556 align:middle
and the beginning of this
year to slow down the pace

22:31.556 --> 22:33.836 align:middle
of increases was to
give yourself time

22:33.836 --> 22:35.746 align:middle
to study the effects
of those moves.

22:36.486 --> 22:39.506 align:middle
After the bank failures in
March, as you've discussed,

22:39.506 --> 22:41.986 align:middle
the Fed staff projected a
recession starting later

22:41.986 --> 22:42.466 align:middle
this year.

22:42.926 --> 22:45.446 align:middle
So my question is
why it was necessary

22:45.446 --> 22:46.826 align:middle
to raise interest rates today.

22:46.826 --> 22:49.076 align:middle
Or, put, put differently: If
the whole point of slowing

22:49.076 --> 22:52.566 align:middle
down the pace was to see
the effects of your moves,

22:52.636 --> 22:54.656 align:middle
and now you've, for
the last two meetings,

22:54.656 --> 22:56.536 align:middle
been seeing the effects
of those moves,

22:57.046 --> 23:00.226 align:middle
why did the Committee feel it
was necessary to keep moving?

23:00.226 --> 23:00.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:01.446 --> 23:06.306 align:middle
Well, we-the reason
is that we-again,

23:06.306 --> 23:09.006 align:middle
with our monetary policy
we're try-trying to reach

23:09.006 --> 23:12.616 align:middle
and then-and then stay at
a, for an extended period,

23:13.106 --> 23:16.476 align:middle
a level of policy-a policy
stance that's sufficiently

23:16.476 --> 23:18.656 align:middle
restrictive to bring inflation
down to 2 percent over time.

23:19.486 --> 23:22.026 align:middle
And, you know, that's
what we're trying to do

23:22.026 --> 23:23.216 align:middle
with our-with our tool.

23:23.836 --> 23:25.966 align:middle
I think slowing down
was the right move.

23:25.966 --> 23:29.716 align:middle
I, I think it's enabled
us to see more data,

23:29.716 --> 23:32.206 align:middle
and it will continue to do so.

23:32.206 --> 23:38.066 align:middle
So I, I-you know, we really-you
know, we have to balance.

23:38.066 --> 23:41.676 align:middle
We always have to balance the
risk of not doing enough and,

23:41.676 --> 23:44.876 align:middle
and not getting inflation
under control against the risk

23:44.976 --> 23:48.096 align:middle
of maybe slowing down
economic activity too much.

23:48.096 --> 23:49.736 align:middle
And we thought that
this rate hike,

23:49.966 --> 23:55.206 align:middle
along with the meaningful change
in, in our policy statement,

23:55.206 --> 23:56.476 align:middle
was the right way
to balance that.

23:56.476 --> 23:57.176 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

23:57.176 --> 24:00.096 align:middle
And just to follow up, you
know, what you said in response

24:00.096 --> 24:04.076 align:middle
to Howard's question-you'll need
data to accumulate to determine

24:04.146 --> 24:06.956 align:middle
if this is a sufficiently
restrictive stance.

24:07.426 --> 24:09.146 align:middle
Does that data need
to accumulate,

24:09.296 --> 24:11.426 align:middle
or could it accumulate
over a longer period

24:11.806 --> 24:13.966 align:middle
than a six-week intermeeting
cycle?

24:15.076 --> 24:15.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:15.686 --> 24:18.476 align:middle
Yeah. I mean, as I
mentioned, I would just say

24:18.476 --> 24:21.406 align:middle
that this assessment
will be an ongoing one.

24:21.406 --> 24:26.076 align:middle
You-you know, you can't-with,
with economic data, you,

24:26.166 --> 24:27.646 align:middle
you can't-you've,

24:27.646 --> 24:29.406 align:middle
you've seen-take
inflation for a minute.

24:29.406 --> 24:30.046 align:middle
Look, look back.

24:30.046 --> 24:33.846 align:middle
We've seen inflation come
down-move back up two

24:33.846 --> 24:35.976 align:middle
or three times since
March of 2021.

24:35.976 --> 24:38.406 align:middle
We've seen inflation have
a few months of coming down

24:38.406 --> 24:39.386 align:middle
and then come right back up.

24:39.386 --> 24:42.976 align:middle
So I think you're going to
want to see that-you know,

24:43.696 --> 24:47.056 align:middle
that a few months of data
will, will persuade you

24:47.056 --> 24:49.616 align:middle
that you've-that you've got
this right, kind of thing.

24:49.946 --> 24:52.316 align:middle
And, you know, we,
we have the luxury:

24:52.316 --> 24:54.206 align:middle
We've raised 500 basis points.

24:54.676 --> 24:56.266 align:middle
I think that policy is tight.

24:56.266 --> 24:58.946 align:middle
I think real rates are
probably-that you can calculate

24:58.946 --> 24:59.856 align:middle
them many different ways.

24:59.856 --> 25:02.146 align:middle
But one way is to look
at the nominal rate

25:02.146 --> 25:05.496 align:middle
and then subtract a, a
reasonable estimate of, of,

25:05.496 --> 25:07.826 align:middle
let's say, one-year inflation,
which might be 3 percent.

25:07.826 --> 25:09.316 align:middle
So you've got 2 percent
real rates.

25:09.316 --> 25:12.656 align:middle
That's meaningfully above what
most people would-many people,

25:12.656 --> 25:15.996 align:middle
anyway, would, would assess
as, you know, the neutral rate.

25:15.996 --> 25:16.986 align:middle
So policy is tight.

25:17.336 --> 25:20.816 align:middle
And you see that in
interest-interest-sensitive

25:21.486 --> 25:22.176 align:middle
activities.

25:22.176 --> 25:23.706 align:middle
And you also begin
to see it more

25:23.706 --> 25:25.396 align:middle
and more in, in other
activities.

25:25.396 --> 25:28.676 align:middle
And if you-if you put the-you
put the credit tightening on top

25:28.676 --> 25:32.506 align:middle
of that and the QT that's,
that's ongoing, I think,

25:32.506 --> 25:34.856 align:middle
I think you feel
like, you know, we're,

25:35.036 --> 25:38.326 align:middle
we may not be far off-we're
possibly even at-that level.

25:38.826 --> 25:40.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:41.836 --> 25:42.766 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

25:44.516 --> 25:46.116 align:middle
Thank you very much,
Chair Powell.

25:46.446 --> 25:47.866 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

25:47.866 --> 25:51.676 align:middle
So if the Federal Reserve gets
down to the 3 percent inflation,

25:51.676 --> 25:54.456 align:middle
as the projections show, at the
end of this year or close to it,

25:54.866 --> 25:57.736 align:middle
would it be okay for you
for a prolonged period

25:57.736 --> 26:01.076 align:middle
of 3 percent inflation and
hoping for some outside event

26:01.076 --> 26:02.876 align:middle
to move down to 2
percent target?

26:04.076 --> 26:04.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:05.066 --> 26:06.016 align:middle
Look-I think we're always going

26:06.016 --> 26:08.016 align:middle
to have 2 percent
as our, our target.

26:08.016 --> 26:10.246 align:middle
We're always going to be
focusing on getting there.

26:10.646 --> 26:12.036 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:12.036 --> 26:13.846 align:middle
You would be okay with
a prolonged 3 percent?

26:13.846 --> 26:14.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:14.146 --> 26:16.886 align:middle
You know, it's-let me just say,

26:16.886 --> 26:18.046 align:middle
that's not what we're
looking for.

26:18.046 --> 26:19.246 align:middle
We're looking for
inflation going

26:19.246 --> 26:21.426 align:middle
down to 2 percent over time.

26:21.646 --> 26:24.816 align:middle
I mean, we-that's, that's not a
question that's in front of us,

26:24.816 --> 26:26.626 align:middle
and it would depend on
so many other things.

26:26.626 --> 26:29.276 align:middle
But, ultimately, we're not
looking to get to 3 percent

26:29.276 --> 26:30.626 align:middle
and then drop our tools.

26:30.626 --> 26:32.986 align:middle
We have a, a goal of
getting to 2 percent.

26:33.546 --> 26:34.946 align:middle
We think it's going
to take some time.

26:34.946 --> 26:36.726 align:middle
We don't think it'll
be a smooth process.

26:37.376 --> 26:40.596 align:middle
And, you know, I think we're
going to-we're going to need

26:40.596 --> 26:42.136 align:middle
to stay at this for a while.

26:42.496 --> 26:42.846 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:42.846 --> 26:43.246 align:middle
How does the other side

26:43.246 --> 26:45.556 align:middle
of the mandate-the jobs
side-once you get to 3 percent,

26:45.806 --> 26:47.446 align:middle
going from 3 to 2, how
does the other side

26:47.446 --> 26:48.746 align:middle
of the mandate balance?

26:48.746 --> 26:49.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:49.426 --> 26:51.066 align:middle
I think they, they-you
know, they,

26:51.066 --> 26:52.946 align:middle
they will both matter
equally at that point.

26:53.106 --> 26:55.566 align:middle
Right now, you have a
labor market that's still

26:55.566 --> 26:56.596 align:middle
extraordinarily tight.

26:57.076 --> 26:59.076 align:middle
You still got 1.6 job openings,

26:59.076 --> 27:00.826 align:middle
even with the lower
job openings number,

27:01.186 --> 27:02.766 align:middle
for every unemployed person.

27:03.336 --> 27:06.226 align:middle
We do see some evidence
of softening

27:06.226 --> 27:07.196 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

27:07.196 --> 27:10.296 align:middle
But, overall, you're near a
50 year low in unemployment.

27:10.896 --> 27:13.566 align:middle
Wages-you all will have
seen that the wage number

27:13.566 --> 27:17.726 align:middle
from late last week, and
it's-whenever it was.

27:17.726 --> 27:20.816 align:middle
And, you know, it's, it's a
couple of percentage points

27:20.816 --> 27:23.086 align:middle
above what would be-what
would be consistent

27:23.086 --> 27:24.856 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation
over time.

27:24.856 --> 27:28.046 align:middle
So we do see some softening; we
see new labor supply coming in.

27:28.046 --> 27:29.476 align:middle
These are very positive
developments.

27:30.246 --> 27:32.166 align:middle
But the labor market
is very, very strong,

27:32.416 --> 27:35.516 align:middle
whereas inflation is, you
know, running high-well

27:35.516 --> 27:37.326 align:middle
above our-well above our goal.

27:37.326 --> 27:39.016 align:middle
And right now, we
need to be focusing

27:39.016 --> 27:40.036 align:middle
on bringing inflation down.

27:40.576 --> 27:42.396 align:middle
Fortunately, we've been
able to do that so far

27:42.396 --> 27:44.206 align:middle
without unemployment going up.

27:44.996 --> 27:46.236 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:46.836 --> 27:48.036 align:middle
Matt. MATTHEW BOESLER.

27:49.166 --> 27:50.096 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:50.096 --> 27:51.526 align:middle
Matthew Boesler with
Bloomberg News.

27:51.526 --> 27:55.176 align:middle
So, many analysts noted at the
time of the March FOMC meeting

27:55.176 --> 27:59.206 align:middle
that at least half of Fed
officials' projections did imply

27:59.206 --> 28:01.326 align:middle
or seemed to imply
that a recession was

28:01.716 --> 28:03.326 align:middle
in their baseline
forecast as well,

28:03.326 --> 28:06.826 align:middle
given the strong first-quarter
GDP tracking estimates.

28:07.296 --> 28:11.326 align:middle
And so I'm just wondering if
you could kind of elaborate on,

28:11.326 --> 28:14.306 align:middle
you know, why you're optimistic
that a recession can be avoided,

28:14.306 --> 28:18.216 align:middle
given that that's the Fed
staff's forecast-possibly also

28:18.216 --> 28:21.826 align:middle
the broader Committee's
forecast as well-and, and also,

28:22.166 --> 28:25.106 align:middle
of course, most private-sector
forecasters'.

28:25.106 --> 28:25.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:25.456 --> 28:28.066 align:middle
Yeah. I don't think-you
know, I know what's printed

28:28.066 --> 28:30.106 align:middle
in the Summary of Economic
Projections and all that.

28:30.106 --> 28:33.316 align:middle
I don't think you can
deduce exactly what you said

28:33.316 --> 28:34.626 align:middle
about what participants think,

28:34.626 --> 28:35.886 align:middle
because you don't know
what they were thinking

28:35.886 --> 28:38.676 align:middle
for first-quarter
GDP at that point.

28:38.676 --> 28:40.726 align:middle
They could have been thinking
about a fairly low number.

28:40.816 --> 28:44.076 align:middle
Anyway, in any case, I'll just
say, I, I continue to think

28:44.076 --> 28:48.316 align:middle
that it's possible that this
time is really different.

28:48.316 --> 28:52.826 align:middle
And the reason is, there's
just so much excess demand,

28:52.826 --> 28:54.026 align:middle
really, in the labor market.

28:54.626 --> 28:56.846 align:middle
It's, it's interesting, as,
you know, we've raised rates

28:56.886 --> 28:59.966 align:middle
by 5 percentage points
in 14 months,

29:00.226 --> 29:02.016 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate is 3½ percent,

29:02.436 --> 29:03.836 align:middle
pretty much where
it was-even lower

29:03.836 --> 29:04.996 align:middle
than where it was
when we started.

29:05.036 --> 29:07.866 align:middle
So job openings are
still very, very high.

29:08.276 --> 29:11.476 align:middle
We see-by surveys and much,
much [other] evidence-that,

29:11.616 --> 29:14.926 align:middle
that conditions are
cooling gradually.

29:15.026 --> 29:16.396 align:middle
But it's-it really is different.

29:16.396 --> 29:18.876 align:middle
You know, it wasn't supposed
to be possible for job openings

29:18.876 --> 29:21.506 align:middle
to decline by as much of
the-as they've declined

29:21.946 --> 29:23.096 align:middle
without unemployment going up.

29:23.096 --> 29:24.236 align:middle
Well, that's what we've seen.

29:24.656 --> 29:27.896 align:middle
So we-there are no
promises in this.

29:27.946 --> 29:30.426 align:middle
But it just seems that,
to me, that it's possible

29:30.426 --> 29:33.126 align:middle
that we can continue to have
a cooling in the labor market

29:33.786 --> 29:37.916 align:middle
without having the big increases
in unemployment that have gone

29:37.916 --> 29:40.746 align:middle
with many, you know,
prior episodes.

29:40.746 --> 29:42.016 align:middle
Now, that would be
against history.

29:42.016 --> 29:43.396 align:middle
I, I fully appreciate that.

29:43.396 --> 29:44.846 align:middle
That would be against
the, the pattern.

29:45.296 --> 29:48.746 align:middle
But I do think that it-that
this, that the situation

29:48.746 --> 29:52.366 align:middle
in the labor market, with
so much excess demand, yet,

29:52.796 --> 29:55.236 align:middle
you know, wages are
actually-wages have been

29:55.236 --> 29:55.726 align:middle
moving down.

29:55.726 --> 29:57.246 align:middle
Wage increases have
been moving down,

29:57.396 --> 30:00.956 align:middle
and that's a good sign-down
to a more sustainable level.

30:00.956 --> 30:03.126 align:middle
So I think that-I think
it's still possible.

30:03.126 --> 30:07.096 align:middle
I-you know, I think, you
know, the, the case of,

30:07.096 --> 30:11.316 align:middle
of avoiding a recession is, in
my view, more likely than that

30:11.316 --> 30:12.646 align:middle
of having, having a recession.

30:13.326 --> 30:14.976 align:middle
But it's not-it's
not that the case

30:14.976 --> 30:17.466 align:middle
of having a recession is-I
don't rule that out, either.

30:17.466 --> 30:20.156 align:middle
It's, it's possible that we
will have what I hope would be a

30:20.156 --> 30:20.866 align:middle
mild recession.

30:20.966 --> 30:21.606 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

30:21.606 --> 30:22.846 align:middle
The Committee also
noted in March

30:22.896 --> 30:26.216 align:middle
that wage growth was
still well above levels

30:26.216 --> 30:28.036 align:middle
that would be consistent
with 2 percent inflation.

30:28.036 --> 30:29.686 align:middle
Do you see that as well?

30:29.686 --> 30:31.396 align:middle
And could you kind
of explain, you know,

30:31.396 --> 30:32.696 align:middle
how you come to that judgment?

30:32.696 --> 30:32.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:33.216 --> 30:37.016 align:middle
Sure. So we look at a range
of wage, wage measures.

30:37.606 --> 30:39.686 align:middle
And then that's in nominal.

30:39.686 --> 30:41.746 align:middle
And then-so you assume
wages should be equal

30:41.746 --> 30:44.696 align:middle
to productivity increases
plus inflation.

30:45.306 --> 30:47.206 align:middle
And so you can-you
can look at, you know,

30:47.206 --> 30:50.226 align:middle
the employment compensation
index, average hourly earnings,

30:50.226 --> 30:53.546 align:middle
the Atlanta wage tracker,
compensation per hour-basically,

30:53.546 --> 30:54.806 align:middle
those four and many others.

30:55.076 --> 30:59.626 align:middle
And you can-you can look at, at
what the-what they would have

30:59.626 --> 31:04.146 align:middle
to run at over a long period of
time for that to be consistent

31:04.146 --> 31:05.126 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

31:05.156 --> 31:05.986 align:middle
They can deviate.

31:06.326 --> 31:08.456 align:middle
You know, corporate
margins can go up and down.

31:08.456 --> 31:11.096 align:middle
And there is a feature
of long expansions

31:11.096 --> 31:13.866 align:middle
where they do go down-where
labor gets a bigger share toward

31:13.866 --> 31:16.646 align:middle
later, later in a
recession-sorry,

31:16.646 --> 31:17.966 align:middle
later in an expansion.

31:18.366 --> 31:22.606 align:middle
So, yeah. The, the-you know, and
we calculate those, and you have

31:22.606 --> 31:26.016 align:middle
to take the precision with a
degree of-a degree of salt.

31:26.116 --> 31:31.346 align:middle
But I would say that what they
will show is that, you know, if,

31:31.346 --> 31:36.226 align:middle
if the-if wages are running at
5 percent, 3 percent is closer

31:36.226 --> 31:37.076 align:middle
to where they need to be.

31:37.076 --> 31:39.956 align:middle
Wage increases and closer
to 3 percent, roughly,

31:40.456 --> 31:42.936 align:middle
is what it would take to get-to
be consistent with inflation

31:42.936 --> 31:46.246 align:middle
over a longer period of time.

31:46.886 --> 31:50.466 align:middle
I-by the way, I don't
want to-I do not think

31:50.466 --> 31:52.516 align:middle
that wages are the
principal driver of inflation.

31:52.516 --> 31:54.006 align:middle
You're asking me a
very specific question.

31:54.056 --> 31:55.016 align:middle
I think there are many things.

31:55.016 --> 31:57.036 align:middle
I think wages and prices
tend to move together.

31:57.736 --> 31:59.666 align:middle
And it's very hard to
say what's causing what.

31:59.736 --> 32:03.046 align:middle
But, you know, I've never
said that, you know,

32:03.266 --> 32:05.336 align:middle
that-that wages are really
the principal driver,

32:05.336 --> 32:06.816 align:middle
because I don't think
that's really right.

32:08.136 --> 32:08.546 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

32:09.026 --> 32:11.136 align:middle
Great. Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

32:11.276 --> 32:12.776 align:middle
Well, you mentioned
profit margins.

32:13.586 --> 32:16.426 align:middle
Those have expanded-did
expand sharply during this

32:16.426 --> 32:17.816 align:middle
inflationary period.

32:17.816 --> 32:19.706 align:middle
And while there are some
signs that they are starting

32:19.706 --> 32:23.296 align:middle
to decline, many economists
note they haven't fallen as much

32:23.296 --> 32:25.486 align:middle
as might be expected,
given that we're seeing

32:25.486 --> 32:27.406 align:middle
at least some pullback
among consumer spending.

32:27.726 --> 32:29.926 align:middle
So, speaking of causes
of inflation,

32:29.926 --> 32:31.786 align:middle
do you see expanded
profit margins

32:31.786 --> 32:33.606 align:middle
as a driver of higher prices?

32:33.986 --> 32:36.886 align:middle
And if so, would you expect
them to narrow soon and,

32:37.166 --> 32:39.876 align:middle
and contribute to reduced
inflation in the coming months?

32:39.876 --> 32:40.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:40.406 --> 32:43.076 align:middle
So higher profits and higher
margins are what happens

32:43.206 --> 32:46.016 align:middle
when you have an imbalance
between supply and demand:

32:46.096 --> 32:48.216 align:middle
too much demand,
not enough supply.

32:48.706 --> 32:51.466 align:middle
And we've been in a situation in
many parts of the economy where,

32:52.006 --> 32:55.006 align:middle
where supply has been fixed
or, or not flexible enough.

32:55.446 --> 32:58.716 align:middle
And so, you know, the
way the market clears is

32:58.746 --> 32:59.576 align:middle
through higher prices.

33:00.016 --> 33:06.666 align:middle
So to get, I, I think-as goods
pipelines have, have gotten,

33:07.226 --> 33:10.926 align:middle
you know, back to normal so that
we don't have the long waits

33:10.926 --> 33:12.066 align:middle
and the shortages and that kind

33:12.066 --> 33:16.436 align:middle
of thing-I think you will
see inflation come down.

33:16.436 --> 33:18.376 align:middle
And you'll see-you'll see
corporate margins coming

33:18.376 --> 33:21.416 align:middle
down as a result of
return of full competition,

33:21.416 --> 33:23.806 align:middle
where there's enough
supply to meet demand.

33:23.806 --> 33:24.546 align:middle
And then it's-then it's,

33:24.876 --> 33:26.486 align:middle
then you're really back
to full competition.

33:26.876 --> 33:29.126 align:middle
That's-that would be the
dynamic I would expect.

33:30.326 --> 33:30.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:30.886 --> 33:33.006 align:middle
Michael. MICHAEL MCKEE.

33:33.436 --> 33:36.096 align:middle
Michael McKee for Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

33:36.466 --> 33:37.766 align:middle
Can you tell us something

33:37.766 --> 33:41.736 align:middle
about what your policy
reaction function is-your policy

33:41.736 --> 33:43.546 align:middle
framework is going forward?

33:43.546 --> 33:45.936 align:middle
When you look at the
economy at the next meeting,

33:45.936 --> 33:49.606 align:middle
are you looking at
incoming data, which is,

33:49.636 --> 33:51.026 align:middle
by definition, backward looking?

33:51.026 --> 33:54.316 align:middle
Are you going to be forecasting
what you think is going

33:54.316 --> 33:54.846 align:middle
to happen?

33:55.216 --> 33:59.336 align:middle
Are you ruling out the rate cuts
that the market has priced in?

33:59.846 --> 34:00.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:01.166 --> 34:02.456 align:middle
I didn't catch the last part.

34:02.456 --> 34:03.026 align:middle
Rolling? MICHAEL MCKEE.

34:03.026 --> 34:05.486 align:middle
Markets have priced in rate
cuts by the end of the year.

34:05.486 --> 34:06.206 align:middle
Do you rule that out?

34:06.206 --> 34:06.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:06.436 --> 34:07.376 align:middle
Oh, yes. Sorry, sorry, sorry.

34:07.596 --> 34:08.086 align:middle
Okay, I got it.

34:08.086 --> 34:09.166 align:middle
So, what are we looking at?

34:09.166 --> 34:13.076 align:middle
I mean, we look at a combination
of data and, and forecasts.

34:13.076 --> 34:14.786 align:middle
Of course, the whole
idea is to-is

34:14.786 --> 34:17.546 align:middle
to create a good forecast based
on what you see in the data.

34:17.886 --> 34:20.096 align:middle
So we're always,
always looking at both.

34:20.626 --> 34:23.016 align:middle
You know, and it will-of course,
it'll be the obvious things.

34:23.016 --> 34:24.806 align:middle
It'll be readings on inflation.

34:24.806 --> 34:27.876 align:middle
It'll be readings on
wages, on economic growth,

34:28.516 --> 34:33.136 align:middle
on the labor market, and
all of those many things.

34:33.136 --> 34:38.026 align:middle
I think a particular focus for
us going-now, over the past six,

34:38.026 --> 34:41.816 align:middle
seven weeks now and going
forward is going to be,

34:41.916 --> 34:44.456 align:middle
what's happening with
credit tightening?

34:45.126 --> 34:48.726 align:middle
Are small and medium-sized banks
tightening credit standards,

34:49.346 --> 34:53.216 align:middle
and, and is that having an
effect on, on loans, on lending?

34:53.576 --> 34:58.046 align:middle
And, you know, so we can
begin to assess how that fits

34:58.046 --> 34:59.156 align:middle
in with monetary policy.

34:59.256 --> 35:00.676 align:middle
That'll, that'll be
an important thing.

35:01.056 --> 35:02.866 align:middle
I just-you know, we'll
be looking at everything.

35:03.306 --> 35:06.436 align:middle
It's-again, I would just
point out, we've raised rates

35:06.486 --> 35:08.236 align:middle
by 5 percentage points.

35:08.786 --> 35:10.426 align:middle
We are shrinking
the balance sheet.

35:11.036 --> 35:14.076 align:middle
And now we have credit
conditions tightening-not just

35:14.076 --> 35:16.156 align:middle
in the normal way, but
perhaps a little bit more,

35:16.156 --> 35:17.626 align:middle
due to what's happened.

35:18.176 --> 35:20.436 align:middle
And we have to factor
all of that in and,

35:20.436 --> 35:24.346 align:middle
and make our assessment
of-you know,

35:24.346 --> 35:27.026 align:middle
of whether our policy stance
is sufficiently restrictive.

35:27.696 --> 35:30.356 align:middle
And we have to do that in
a world where policy works

35:30.356 --> 35:31.636 align:middle
with long and variable lags.

35:32.126 --> 35:33.086 align:middle
So this is challenging.

35:33.326 --> 35:35.516 align:middle
But, you know, we, we will
make our best assessment,

35:35.586 --> 35:36.766 align:middle
and that's what we think.

35:36.766 --> 35:37.376 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

35:37.376 --> 35:39.556 align:middle
What about the idea
of rate cuts?

35:39.636 --> 35:40.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:40.316 --> 35:46.916 align:middle
Yeah. So we on the
Committee have a-have a view

35:47.896 --> 35:53.196 align:middle
that inflation is going to
come down-not so quickly,

35:53.196 --> 35:54.706 align:middle
but it'll take some time.

35:55.646 --> 35:58.236 align:middle
And in that world, if that
forecast is broadly right,

35:58.326 --> 36:00.916 align:middle
it would not be appropriate
and-to cut rates,

36:00.916 --> 36:01.936 align:middle
and we won't cut rates.

36:01.936 --> 36:04.886 align:middle
If you have a different
forecast and, you know,

36:04.886 --> 36:08.046 align:middle
markets-or have been, from time
to time, pricing in, you know,

36:08.046 --> 36:11.096 align:middle
quite rapid reductions
in inflation, you know,

36:12.016 --> 36:13.276 align:middle
we'd, we'd factor that in.

36:13.276 --> 36:14.576 align:middle
But that's not our forecast.

36:14.726 --> 36:16.116 align:middle
And, of course, the history

36:16.116 --> 36:18.066 align:middle
of the last two years
has been very much

36:18.666 --> 36:20.356 align:middle
that inflation moves down.

36:20.356 --> 36:24.126 align:middle
Particularly now, if you look at
nonhousing services, it really,

36:24.606 --> 36:26.386 align:middle
really hasn't moved much.

36:27.016 --> 36:28.316 align:middle
And it's quite stable.

36:28.316 --> 36:32.096 align:middle
And, you know, so we think
we'll have to-demand will have

36:32.096 --> 36:34.456 align:middle
to weaken a little bit, and
labor market conditions,

36:34.486 --> 36:36.826 align:middle
conditions may have to
soften a bit more to begin

36:36.826 --> 36:37.766 align:middle
to see progress there.

36:38.066 --> 36:39.346 align:middle
And, again, in that world,

36:39.346 --> 36:41.886 align:middle
it wouldn't be-it wouldn't be
appropriate for us to cut rates.

36:43.616 --> 36:44.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:46.226 --> 36:46.456 align:middle
Courtenay.

36:47.116 --> 36:48.016 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

36:48.016 --> 36:49.856 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

36:49.976 --> 36:52.056 align:middle
I'm curious how you
view the role

36:52.056 --> 36:55.306 align:middle
of the overnight reverse
repo facility in the context

36:55.306 --> 36:57.066 align:middle
of the current banking stress.

36:57.346 --> 36:58.916 align:middle
Do you think it's
contributing to the stress

36:58.916 --> 37:02.546 align:middle
by making it more attractive for
money market funds to compete

37:02.546 --> 37:03.596 align:middle
with banks for deposits?

37:04.136 --> 37:07.386 align:middle
And did the Committee
discuss any changes

37:07.416 --> 37:09.016 align:middle
to the structure
of the facility?

37:09.346 --> 37:11.556 align:middle
Or do you see that being put
on the table in the future?

37:11.886 --> 37:12.446 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

37:12.446 --> 37:15.656 align:middle
Sure. So we looked at that very
carefully, as you would imagine.

37:15.656 --> 37:17.116 align:middle
And, you know, the-it's,

37:17.866 --> 37:19.826 align:middle
it's really not contributing,
we don't think, now.

37:19.826 --> 37:21.066 align:middle
It hasn't actually been growing.

37:21.176 --> 37:22.526 align:middle
You know, it moved up-moved down

37:22.606 --> 37:24.706 align:middle
and then moved back
up to where it was.

37:25.216 --> 37:27.646 align:middle
What happened in the-when,

37:27.676 --> 37:29.566 align:middle
when there were the big deposit
flows, which, by the way,

37:29.566 --> 37:32.656 align:middle
have really stabilized
now-what happened was,

37:32.856 --> 37:36.026 align:middle
institutional investors took
their uninsured deposits

37:36.026 --> 37:38.956 align:middle
and put them in government money
market funds, which bought paper

37:38.956 --> 37:40.796 align:middle
from the Federal Home Loan
Banks and things like that.

37:41.386 --> 37:46.226 align:middle
Over the course of, of
maybe the last year,

37:46.296 --> 37:48.846 align:middle
retail investors had
been gradual-as they do

37:48.846 --> 37:50.486 align:middle
in every tightening cycle,

37:50.766 --> 37:52.776 align:middle
they've been gradually
moving their deposits

37:53.236 --> 37:57.136 align:middle
into higher-yielding places such
as CDs and, and other things,

37:57.136 --> 37:58.266 align:middle
including money market funds.

37:58.466 --> 38:01.436 align:middle
So that's a gradual process
that is quite natural

38:01.436 --> 38:03.896 align:middle
and happens during a,
a tightening cycle.

38:03.896 --> 38:06.096 align:middle
What was unusual, really,

38:06.096 --> 38:08.776 align:middle
was the institutional investors
moving their uninsured deposits

38:08.776 --> 38:10.536 align:middle
and spreading them around
and things like that.

38:10.576 --> 38:13.646 align:middle
But it doesn't seem to have
had any-any effect overall

38:13.726 --> 38:16.406 align:middle
on the overnight repo facility.

38:16.956 --> 38:21.166 align:middle
That is really there to,
to help us keep rates

38:21.166 --> 38:22.736 align:middle
where they're supposed to
be, and it's, it's serving

38:22.736 --> 38:24.156 align:middle
that purpose very well.

38:25.326 --> 38:26.996 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:27.076 --> 38:30.316 align:middle
Sarah. SARAH EWALL-WICE.

38:30.316 --> 38:31.086 align:middle
Sarah Ewall-Wice, CBS News.

38:31.086 --> 38:33.066 align:middle
I want to go back to the
debt ceiling for a moment.

38:33.066 --> 38:35.536 align:middle
I know you talked about that
in terms of fiscal policy,

38:35.826 --> 38:38.216 align:middle
but can you just speak
towards what the impact

38:38.216 --> 38:41.026 align:middle
of a default would mean for
Americans across the country,

38:41.276 --> 38:42.526 align:middle
the markets, and borrowing?

38:44.936 --> 38:45.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:45.846 --> 38:47.166 align:middle
Yeah. I would just say, it's -I,

38:47.936 --> 38:50.916 align:middle
I don't really think we should
be-we shouldn't even be talking

38:50.916 --> 38:53.006 align:middle
about a world in which the
U.S. doesn't pay its bills.

38:53.446 --> 38:55.886 align:middle
It just-it just shouldn't
be a thing.

38:55.886 --> 38:57.856 align:middle
And, and again, I
would just say,

38:57.856 --> 39:00.276 align:middle
we don't-no one should assume

39:00.276 --> 39:04.686 align:middle
that the Fed can do-can
really protect the economy

39:04.686 --> 39:08.176 align:middle
and the financial system and
our reputation globally from,

39:08.336 --> 39:11.976 align:middle
from the damage that such,
such an event might inflict.

39:13.116 --> 39:14.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:15.196 --> 39:20.006 align:middle
Scott. SCOTT HORSLEY.

39:20.836 --> 39:21.566 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

39:21.566 --> 39:22.516 align:middle
Scott Horsley for NPR.

39:23.046 --> 39:25.776 align:middle
In his report last week, Vice
Chair Barr identified a couple

39:25.776 --> 39:27.566 align:middle
of the factors that
he thought contributed

39:27.566 --> 39:30.146 align:middle
to the regulatory-or
the supervisory lapses

39:30.146 --> 39:34.206 align:middle
at Silicon Valley Bank: a policy
change in 2019 to exempt all

39:34.206 --> 39:36.016 align:middle
but the biggest banks
from strict scrutiny

39:36.506 --> 39:38.236 align:middle
and also what he called a sort

39:38.236 --> 39:42.246 align:middle
of cultural shift towards
less aggressive oversight.

39:43.266 --> 39:44.396 align:middle
You were here in 2019.

39:44.396 --> 39:47.426 align:middle
Do you share that view, and
what would it take to get the,

39:47.666 --> 39:50.226 align:middle
the stronger oversight
that you and he said

39:50.226 --> 39:51.526 align:middle
in your release would
be necessary?

39:51.816 --> 39:52.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:52.356 --> 39:56.396 align:middle
So I, I didn't-I didn't take
part in creating the report

39:56.396 --> 39:59.606 align:middle
or doing the work, but I
do-I have read it, of course.

39:59.606 --> 40:02.006 align:middle
And I find it persuasive.

40:02.226 --> 40:08.266 align:middle
I mean, I would say it this way:
A, a very large-a large bank,

40:08.266 --> 40:12.386 align:middle
not a very large bank-a large
bank failed quite suddenly

40:12.666 --> 40:17.016 align:middle
and unexpectedly in a way that
threatened to spread contagion

40:17.016 --> 40:19.816 align:middle
into the financial system.

40:20.346 --> 40:24.916 align:middle
I think the only thing that,
that I'm really focused on is

40:25.056 --> 40:28.276 align:middle
to understand what went
wrong, what happened, and,

40:28.376 --> 40:31.716 align:middle
and identify what we need
to do to address that.

40:32.226 --> 40:36.866 align:middle
Some of that is-may, it may just
have been technology evolving.

40:36.866 --> 40:38.296 align:middle
You know, we have to
keep up with all that.

40:38.296 --> 40:41.426 align:middle
But some of it may be our
policies, and-supervisory

40:41.426 --> 40:43.136 align:middle
and regulatory, whatever.

40:43.436 --> 40:46.896 align:middle
What our job is now is, identify
those things and implement them.

40:47.166 --> 40:48.846 align:middle
And that's kind of
the only thing I care

40:48.846 --> 40:51.326 align:middle
about is-and I think-I
feel like I am accountable

40:51.866 --> 40:54.766 align:middle
for doing everything I can to
make sure that that happens.

40:55.896 --> 40:56.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:56.496 --> 41:00.696 align:middle
Let's go to Evan.

41:00.996 --> 41:01.886 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

41:02.136 --> 41:02.446 align:middle
Thank you.

41:02.446 --> 41:04.516 align:middle
Evan Ryser with MNI Market News.

41:05.186 --> 41:07.236 align:middle
Chair Powell, are we
in the early stage

41:07.336 --> 41:08.746 align:middle
or nearing the end stage

41:08.776 --> 41:10.976 align:middle
of the banking turmoil
among regional banks?

41:11.596 --> 41:15.506 align:middle
And, secondly, do you still
have a bias to tighten rates?

41:15.716 --> 41:20.516 align:middle
Is that what the
statement is saying?

41:20.966 --> 41:21.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:21.846 --> 41:25.526 align:middle
So I guess I would-I guess
I would say it this way.

41:26.756 --> 41:33.556 align:middle
There were three large banks,
really, from the very beginning

41:33.806 --> 41:36.816 align:middle
that were at the heart of the
stress that we-that we saw

41:36.816 --> 41:38.716 align:middle
in early March, the
severe period of stress.

41:39.076 --> 41:40.676 align:middle
Those have now all
been resolved,

41:41.056 --> 41:42.716 align:middle
and all the depositors
have been protected.

41:42.926 --> 41:48.016 align:middle
I think that the resolution and
sale of, of First Republic kind

41:48.016 --> 41:51.436 align:middle
of draws a line under that
period of-is an important step

41:51.506 --> 41:54.666 align:middle
toward drawing a line under that
period of, of severe stress.

41:54.966 --> 42:01.886 align:middle
Okay. I also think we are very
focused on what's happening

42:01.886 --> 42:04.076 align:middle
with credit availability,
particularly, you know,

42:04.076 --> 42:07.966 align:middle
with what you saw in the-in the
Beige Book, and you will see

42:07.966 --> 42:11.436 align:middle
in the SLOOS, is, is small
and medium-medium-sized,

42:11.436 --> 42:15.506 align:middle
small and medium-sized banks
who are feeling that they need

42:15.506 --> 42:17.536 align:middle
to tighten credit
standards-build liquidity.

42:17.536 --> 42:19.426 align:middle
What's going to be the
macroeconomic effect of that?

42:20.376 --> 42:22.716 align:middle
More broadly, we,
we will continue

42:22.716 --> 42:26.096 align:middle
to very carefully monitor what's
going on in the banking system,

42:26.706 --> 42:29.306 align:middle
and we'll factor that
assessment into our decisions

42:29.306 --> 42:30.686 align:middle
in an important way,
going forward.

42:32.226 --> 42:33.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:33.426 --> 42:33.906 align:middle
Okay. Greg.

42:35.466 --> 42:36.636 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

42:36.886 --> 42:38.146 align:middle
Thank you, Fed Chairman.

42:38.636 --> 42:39.876 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

42:40.336 --> 42:43.836 align:middle
I just wondered if you've
done any reflection on,

42:44.316 --> 42:48.556 align:middle
on your own actions during this
crisis and leading up to it

42:48.836 --> 42:50.736 align:middle
over the last-since
you've been Fed Chairman,

42:51.126 --> 42:52.856 align:middle
I think I've heard you
say a couple of times

42:52.856 --> 42:56.966 align:middle
that you deferred to the
Vice Chair for Supervision.

42:57.286 --> 43:01.206 align:middle
Do you think that was the
right way to go about this?

43:01.206 --> 43:03.486 align:middle
And, yeah, comments on that.

43:03.486 --> 43:03.796 align:middle
Thank you.

43:03.876 --> 43:04.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:04.196 --> 43:06.576 align:middle
Sure. So let me say, first
of all, I've been Chair

43:06.576 --> 43:09.636 align:middle
of the Board for five-plus
years now, and I fully recognize

43:09.666 --> 43:11.106 align:middle
that we made mistakes.

43:11.296 --> 43:13.276 align:middle
I think we've learned
some new things as well,

43:13.796 --> 43:14.776 align:middle
and we need to do better.

43:15.006 --> 43:17.826 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, I thought
the report was unflinching

43:17.826 --> 43:18.796 align:middle
and appropriately so.

43:19.306 --> 43:20.466 align:middle
I welcome it.

43:20.976 --> 43:24.236 align:middle
And I agree with and will
support those recommendations.

43:24.236 --> 43:26.856 align:middle
And I do feel that I'm
personally accountable

43:26.856 --> 43:30.956 align:middle
to do what I can
to foster measures

43:30.956 --> 43:33.036 align:middle
that will address the problems.

43:33.796 --> 43:38.146 align:middle
So, on, on the Vice Chair
for Supervision, you know,

43:38.276 --> 43:40.936 align:middle
the place to start is,
is the statutory role,

43:40.936 --> 43:42.126 align:middle
which is quite unusual.

43:42.626 --> 43:45.156 align:middle
The Vice Chair, it says,

43:45.246 --> 43:49.196 align:middle
shall deploy policy
recommendations-"develop policy

43:49.196 --> 43:51.236 align:middle
recommendations for the
Board regarding supervision

43:51.236 --> 43:52.966 align:middle
and regulation of
depository institution .

43:52.966 --> 43:53.446 align:middle
. . companies .

43:54.176 --> 43:56.096 align:middle
. . , and shall oversee
the supervision

43:56.096 --> 43:57.896 align:middle
and regulation of such firms."

43:58.306 --> 44:00.916 align:middle
So this is Congress
establishing a four-year term

44:00.916 --> 44:02.746 align:middle
for someone else
on the Board-not,

44:02.886 --> 44:06.176 align:middle
not the Chair-as Vice Chair
for Supervision who really gets

44:06.176 --> 44:08.836 align:middle
to set the agenda for
supervision and regulation

44:08.876 --> 44:09.656 align:middle
for the Board of Governors.

44:10.066 --> 44:11.426 align:middle
Congress wanted that person

44:12.036 --> 44:14.426 align:middle
to be-to have political
accountability

44:14.426 --> 44:15.616 align:middle
for developing that agenda.

44:15.716 --> 44:18.106 align:middle
So the way it works-the way
it has worked in practice

44:18.106 --> 44:21.136 align:middle
for me is, I've had a
good working relationship.

44:21.316 --> 44:25.556 align:middle
I give my, my counsel,
my input privately,

44:26.606 --> 44:28.616 align:middle
and that's-I offer that.

44:28.706 --> 44:30.666 align:middle
And I have good conversations,

44:30.666 --> 44:32.616 align:middle
and I try to contribute
constructively.

44:32.886 --> 44:35.256 align:middle
I respect the authority
that Congress has deferred

44:35.346 --> 44:38.686 align:middle
on that person, including
working with,

44:38.816 --> 44:41.126 align:middle
with Vice Chair Barr
and, and his predecessor.

44:41.816 --> 44:44.546 align:middle
And I think that's the
way it's supposed to work,

44:44.716 --> 44:45.526 align:middle
and that's appropriate.

44:45.656 --> 44:48.136 align:middle
I, I believe that's
what the law requires.

44:48.196 --> 44:50.246 align:middle
And, you know-but,

44:50.966 --> 44:56.216 align:middle
but it isn't-I wouldn't say it's
a matter of complete deference.

44:56.216 --> 45:00.586 align:middle
It's more, I have a-I have a
role in, in presenting my views

45:00.586 --> 45:02.946 align:middle
and discussing-having an
intelligent discussion

45:02.946 --> 45:04.276 align:middle
about what's going on and why.

45:04.396 --> 45:06.776 align:middle
And, you know, that's,
that's my input.

45:06.836 --> 45:09.356 align:middle
But, ultimately, that person
does get to set the agenda

45:09.626 --> 45:12.626 align:middle
and gets to take things to the
Board of Governors and really,

45:12.626 --> 45:15.386 align:middle
in supervision, has sole
authority over supervision.

45:17.656 --> 45:18.196 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

45:19.636 --> 45:21.556 align:middle
Just wondered if you
have any regrets?

45:21.556 --> 45:24.206 align:middle
Or was there anything
that, you know-decisions

45:24.206 --> 45:26.896 align:middle
that maybe you regret now
in light of what's happened?

45:27.396 --> 45:27.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:28.736 --> 45:31.916 align:middle
I've had a few, sure.

45:31.916 --> 45:34.296 align:middle
I mean, you know, who
doesn't look back and think

45:34.296 --> 45:35.596 align:middle
that you could have
done things differently?

45:35.596 --> 45:37.296 align:middle
But, honestly, you don't-you
don't get to do that.

45:37.296 --> 45:40.586 align:middle
Again, my focus is on
what-control the controllable.

45:40.586 --> 45:43.066 align:middle
As one of my great
mentors used to always say,

45:43.316 --> 45:44.156 align:middle
"Control the controllable."

45:44.156 --> 45:46.536 align:middle
What we control now is,
make a fair assessment,

45:46.596 --> 45:47.666 align:middle
learn the right lessons,

45:48.316 --> 45:50.636 align:middle
figure out what the fixes
are, and implement them.

45:51.206 --> 45:53.936 align:middle
And, and I think that, that
Vice Chair Barr's report is an

45:53.936 --> 46:00.546 align:middle
excellent first step in that,
but we've got to follow through.

46:00.716 --> 46:01.706 align:middle
MEGAN CASSELLA.

46:04.996 --> 46:05.416 align:middle
Hi, there.

46:05.416 --> 46:06.636 align:middle
Megan Cassella with Barron's.

46:07.146 --> 46:09.976 align:middle
Did the possibility of pausing
at this meeting come up at all,

46:09.976 --> 46:11.966 align:middle
and how seriously
was that considered?

46:12.096 --> 46:13.666 align:middle
I'm curious if you
can give us any color

46:13.906 --> 46:15.836 align:middle
as to whether there were
any initial concerns

46:15.836 --> 46:18.456 align:middle
about raising rates again or
what those discussions entailed.

46:18.456 --> 46:19.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:19.866 --> 46:24.746 align:middle
So support for the 25 basis
point rate increase was very

46:24.746 --> 46:26.906 align:middle
strong across the board.

46:29.346 --> 46:31.226 align:middle
I would say there are a number
of people-and, you know,

46:31.226 --> 46:32.206 align:middle
you'll see this in the minutes.

46:32.206 --> 46:36.256 align:middle
I don't want to try to do
the head count in real time.

46:36.406 --> 46:38.796 align:middle
But people did talk about
pausing, but not so much

46:38.796 --> 46:41.466 align:middle
at this meeting, you know, that
we're-I mean, there's a sense

46:41.466 --> 46:44.436 align:middle
that we're, we're-that,
you know, we're much closer

46:44.436 --> 46:46.736 align:middle
to the end of this than to
the beginning, that-you know,

46:46.776 --> 46:48.136 align:middle
as I mentioned, if
you-if you add

46:48.136 --> 46:49.536 align:middle
up all the tightening
that's going

46:49.536 --> 46:52.866 align:middle
on through various
channels, it's-we,

46:52.866 --> 46:54.776 align:middle
we feel like we-you know,

46:54.776 --> 46:56.836 align:middle
we're getting close or,
or maybe even there.

46:57.256 --> 46:59.246 align:middle
But that, again, that's going
to be an ongoing assessment.

46:59.436 --> 47:01.606 align:middle
And, and we're going to be
looking at those factors

47:02.296 --> 47:04.866 align:middle
that we listed and-to
determine whether there's,

47:05.076 --> 47:05.566 align:middle
there's more to do.

47:05.566 --> 47:06.186 align:middle
MEGAN CASSELLA.

47:06.396 --> 47:08.386 align:middle
I'm curious, too, how
to interpret that and,

47:08.386 --> 47:09.696 align:middle
and the changes to
the statement.

47:10.036 --> 47:12.996 align:middle
Is the bar higher now to raise
rates at the next meeting,

47:12.996 --> 47:16.206 align:middle
or would a strong jobs report
or inflation print be enough

47:16.616 --> 47:17.616 align:middle
to push the Fed to
tighten again?

47:17.616 --> 47:18.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:18.826 --> 47:21.256 align:middle
I don't want to-I couldn't-I
couldn't really say.

47:21.256 --> 47:23.736 align:middle
I just think we're-I
think we-look,

47:23.736 --> 47:25.946 align:middle
I think we've moved a
long way fairly quickly.

47:26.306 --> 47:29.836 align:middle
And I think we can afford
to look at the data and,

47:29.836 --> 47:31.806 align:middle
and make a careful assessment.

47:32.936 --> 47:33.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:33.426 --> 47:35.326 align:middle
And we'll go to Nancy
for the last question.

47:35.326 --> 47:37.176 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

47:37.256 --> 47:37.906 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

47:37.906 --> 47:39.886 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

47:40.176 --> 47:43.036 align:middle
You mentioned a few times about
the lessons you've learned

47:43.036 --> 47:46.136 align:middle
from the banking crisis-that you
would learn the right lessons.

47:46.756 --> 47:47.996 align:middle
What are those lessons?

47:48.236 --> 47:49.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:49.556 --> 47:55.836 align:middle
Well, I just would start with
something that's changed,

47:55.836 --> 48:00.216 align:middle
really, which is this-the run
on Silicon Valley Bank was

48:00.706 --> 48:03.406 align:middle
out of keeping with the speed
of runs through history.

48:04.036 --> 48:09.106 align:middle
And that now needs to be
reflected in some-in some way

48:09.156 --> 48:10.836 align:middle
in regulation and
in supervision.

48:11.396 --> 48:14.226 align:middle
It-we know-now that
we know it's possible,

48:14.886 --> 48:17.166 align:middle
I think we didn't-no one
thought that was possible.

48:17.626 --> 48:20.136 align:middle
No one-I'm not aware
of anybody thinking

48:20.136 --> 48:21.916 align:middle
that this could happen
so-quite so quickly.

48:21.916 --> 48:24.776 align:middle
So I think that, you know,
that will play through.

48:24.776 --> 48:28.986 align:middle
I, I, you know-it will
be up to Vice Chair Barr

48:29.726 --> 48:31.636 align:middle
to really take the lead

48:31.636 --> 48:33.976 align:middle
in designing the
ways to address that.

48:33.976 --> 48:35.356 align:middle
But I think-I think
that's one thing.

48:36.016 --> 48:39.076 align:middle
I, I guess I would
just say that.

48:39.076 --> 48:40.386 align:middle
Then, you know-that
we're going to-obviously,

48:40.546 --> 48:42.016 align:middle
we're going to revisit [this].

48:42.056 --> 48:44.666 align:middle
It's pretty clear we need-to
me, anyways-clear that we need

48:44.666 --> 48:47.486 align:middle
to strengthen both
supervision and regulations

48:47.516 --> 48:48.726 align:middle
for banks of this size.

48:49.196 --> 48:51.136 align:middle
And I'm, I'm thinking that we're

48:51.136 --> 48:52.676 align:middle
on the-on track to
do that as well.

48:53.216 --> 48:54.206 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:54.206 --> 48:56.266 align:middle
Can you be any more specific
on stress testing or looking

48:56.366 --> 48:58.526 align:middle
at banks that have
specific concentrations

48:58.646 --> 48:59.776 align:middle
in certain parts of the economy?

48:59.776 --> 49:00.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:00.056 --> 49:00.846 align:middle
Yeah. That's-see, that's,

49:00.906 --> 49:03.456 align:middle
that's what Vice Chair
Barr's role really is,

49:03.456 --> 49:11.176 align:middle
and he'll take the lead on that.

49:11.716 --> 49:18.726 align:middle
Thank you.

